<Layout
    title="strudel.cc w/ Azan"
    description="Notes and material for a strudel.cc workshop"
>
    <style>
        @font-face {
            font-family: "BigBlue Terminal";
            src: url("/BigBlue_TerminalPlus.TTF") format("truetype");
            font-weight: normal;
            font-style: normal;
            font-display: swap;
        }

        :root {
            font-family: "BigBlue Terminal", monospace;
        }
    </style>
    <script src="https://unpkg.com/@strudel/repl@latest"></script>
    <article class="space-y-4">
        <section class="p-4 border mb-8">
            Hello! My name is Azan (a·zaan). I'm a self-taught musician and a
            software engineer. This page is a reference you can return to
            throughout the workshop. Use it to explore different approaches to
            playing sounds and building patterns in Strudel.
            <div class="mt-4">
                <ContactLinks links={CONTACT_LINKS} />
            </div>
        </section>
        <h1>Your first beat!</h1>
        <p>
            The following are some examples of beats I have
            authored, along with others I have repurposed from the <a
                href="https://strudel.cc/workshop/getting-started/"
                >Strudel documentation</a
            >.
        </p>
        <p>
            bd = bass drum, sd = snare drum, rim = rimshot, hh = hihat, oh =
            open hihat, lt = low tom, mt = middle tom, ht = high tom, rd = ride
            cymbal, cr = crash cymbal, cp = clap
        </p>
        <section class="flex flex-col gap-4 mb-8">
            {drums.map((d) => <StrudelEditor code={d} />)}
        </section>
        <h1>Patterns</h1>
        <p></p>
        <p>
            Slow down using <code>/</code>, Alternate using <code>{"<>"}</code>,
            Elongate using <code>@</code>, Replicate using <code>!</code>,
            Parallel using <code>,</code>, Speed up using <code>*</code>,
            Sub-sequence using <code>[]</code>, Rest using <code>~</code>.
        </p>
        <section class="flex flex-col gap-4 mb-8">
            {melodies.map((d) => <StrudelEditor code={d} />)}
        </section>
        <h1>Patches I have written</h1>
        <section class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-8">
            {my_patches.map((d) => <StrudelEditor code={d} />)}
        </section>
        <h1>Patches I have found</h1>
        <p>
            From personal websites, strudel’s Codeberg issues, and friends. I
            like these because each patch shows a different way of thinking
            about and playing with Strudel. The <a
                href="https://strudel.cc/intro/showcase/">strudel.cc Showcase</a
            > is also great to look at.
        </p>
        <section class="flex flex-col gap-4 mb-8">
            {found_patches.map((p) => <StrudelEditor code={p.code} />)}
        </section>
        <h1>Community and playing together!</h1>
        <p>
            I've enjoyed playing with other people using <a
                href="https://flok.cc">flok.cc</a
            >, and sometimes taking turns to write a line on the same computer
            feels much better.
        </p>
        <p>
            Communities like <a href="https://www.instagram.com/algorave_india"
                >Algorave India</a
            >, <a href="https://www.instagram.com/livecode.blr">livecode.blr</a> are
            active in Bangalore. <a href="https://blog.toplap.org/">TOPLAP</a> is
            quite an old organization operating around livecoding and they have an
            active discord server. The <a
                href="https://tidalcycles.org/docs/community/"
                >TidalCycles community</a
            > also has a very active forum and discord server.
        </p>
        <p>Go out there and play!</p>
    </article>
</Layout>
